# Conventions

## Code
- `mesh-core` is **framework-free TypeScript** — no React, no DOM assumptions
  beyond guarded `typeof window`/`document` checks. React lives only in
  `mesh-react` and the apps.
- Pure, deterministic core: planners/topology are pure functions over a roster
  snapshot; inject clocks (`now`) and use deterministic hashes for tie-breaks so
  concurrent peers resolve routes identically and tests don't flake.
- Runtime-validate everything off the wire in `guards.ts` (see the mesh-protocol
  rule — additive idiom).
- Match surrounding style; comments state constraints/rationale, not narration.

## Tests
- `node:test` under `packages/*/test`. Run `npm run test -w @unstable-legion/core`
  (and `-w @unstable-legion/react`). Add a regression test with every wire/guard
  or planner change. `apps/*` e2e is Playwright (GPU-gated, dispatch-only in CI).
- After editing `mesh-core/src`, rebuild its `dist` before the apps consume it.

## Commits + source of truth
- **Commit to GitHub `main`** — the Forgejo repo is a read-only mirror.
- **No AI/Claude attribution** in commit messages or trailers; author as the user.
- Nothing is "done" until committed, merged, AND (for app/infra changes)
  deployed + verified live.
- Persist host/infra changes to `homelab-compose` IaC in the same task.

## Product decisions that are NOT up for casual reversal
- No third-party STUN / signaling brokers (self-hosted only — see infra rule).
- Guards stay additive/lenient (a version-skewed peer must not be dropped).
- The map/read-only subsystems and the mesh engine stay separated where noted.
